carla: Quick sync: I'm consolidating the Python/TS/Go examples for streaming + pagination + retries so the quickstarts aren't out of sync. Drafting a PR with updated snippets and a short migration note. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/3560 :memo:

nate: Nice. Which cursor name are we standardizing on? We still see `cursor`, `next_cursor`, and `page_token` across examples.

carla: I'm proposing `cursor_token` (opaque string) for the docs to make it explicit that it's opaque and token-like. Will add a line: "Do not parse the cursor_token; treat as opaque." Also include a tiny client loop pattern for each language.

isabel: Good. For TS let's show both async iterator and stream reader patterns plus a small exponential backoff util with jitter. Example gist: 

```ts
async function backoff<T>(fn: ()=>Promise<T>, attempts=3){
  for(let i=0;i<attempts;i++){
    try { return await fn() }
    catch(e){ if(i===attempts-1) throw e; const wait = Math.pow(2,i)*100 + Math.random()*50; await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r, wait)) }
  }
}

// iterator style
for await (const part of client.chat.stream({model, input})) console.log(part.text)

// reader style
const reader = client.chat.stream({model, input}).getReader()
while(true){ const {done, value} = await reader.read(); if(done) break; console.log(value.text) }
```

omar: For Go I'd prefer a short SSE-like loop using bufio.Scanner and a tiny retry wrapper that accepts a func returning (*http.Response, error). Keep it copy/pasteable. Also show context cancellation example.

carla: Python plan: show an async generator usage with resume token storage (last_event_id) and a reconnect-on-transient pattern that re-requests using resume_from=last_event_id. Keep the snippet small and explain resume is best-effort.

isabel: We should also call out one thing: if a client caches pages and later switches to cursor_token pagination, there can be off-by-one/missing items due to eventual consistency. Add a short "how to reconcile" section recommending a backfill or a short windowed re-fetch strategy.

nate: Can you include a tiny pagination loop example for each language that uses cursor_token? e.g. TS: 

```ts
let cursor: string | undefined = undefined
while(true){
  const resp = await client.items.list({limit: 100, cursor_token: cursor})
  for(const it of resp.items) handle(it)
  if(!resp.cursor_token) break
  cursor = resp.cursor_token
}
```

carla: Yep. I'll do Python/TS/Go versions and mark the helper functions as optional so teams can replace with their infra helpers. Also adding a short note: "Do not rely on cursor_token ordering; use item timestamps if you need deterministic ordering."

docs-bot: Draft PR opened by carla: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/3560  labels: docs, examples, migration

omar: One tiny ask: for the TS reader example, add an example of handling partial JSON pieces (teams often need to accumulate chunks into a buffer).

carla: Good call. I'll add a 2-line accumulation example and a comment about using structured streaming when available. Planning to request reviews from @isabel and @omar later today.

isabel: I'll review, focusing on the backoff wording and the jitter numbers. Small nit: prefer `cursor_token` in examples but call out legacy aliases in migration note.

carla: Will include a short backport checklist for the archived quickstarts (v0.7/v0.8) and add a one-liner to the migration guide. Thanks all :heart:
